%S A402125 1,2,1,3,2,1,4,5,2,1,3,6,7,2,1,8,4,9,3,2,1,5,10,11,12,13,2,1,6,3,4,14,
%T A402125 7,15,2,1,16,17,5,8,18,3,19,2,1,9,20,4,21,22,6,23,24,2,1,10,3,25,7,5,
%U A402125 11,26,12,27,2,1,13,4,28,29,8,30,3,31,32,33,2,1,6,14,34,9,35,5,15,36,37,38
%N A402125 Card read at step n if, at each step, the top card of the deck 1, 2, 3, ... is read, removed and put back below n - k of the remaining cards, where k is the last earlier step at which this card was read (k = 0 if there is none).
%C A402125 Start with an infinite deck of cards labeled 1, 2, 3, ..., with card 1 on top. At step n = 1, 2, 3, ..., read the top card, remove it, and put it back so that exactly n - k of the remaining cards are above it, where k is the last earlier step at which this card was read (k = 0 if it was not read before). So a card goes back as deep as its age n - k, the number of steps since it was last read. a(n) is the label of the card read at step n. The first eight steps are worked out in the Example section.
%C A402125 Every card is read infinitely often, but the gaps between its readings keep growing, and for each fixed card the proportion of steps at which it is read tends to 0: card 1 is read exactly at the steps 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, ... (the triangular numbers, A000217), card 2 exactly at the steps 2, 5, 9, 14, 20, ... (A000096), and no card is read more than (sqrt(8*t+1) - 1)/2 times in the first t steps. On the other hand, 39952473 of the first 10^8 steps show a card that was not read before.
%C A402125 Cards are read for the first time in the order 1, 2, 3, ..., so the number of distinct cards read in the first t steps is K(t) = max(a(1), ..., a(t)) for t >= 1. The only possible limit of the proportion K(t)/t of steps that show a new card is the constant lambda = 0.39952466709679946... (A268107), which is defined as follows. For real x >= 0 let H_0 = 0, H_1 = x and H_(r+1) = H_r*(1 + H_r - H_(r-1)) for r >= 1. If x < lambda, the numbers H_r converge to a limit less than 1; if x = lambda, they converge to 1; if x > lambda, they tend to infinity. It is proved that liminf K(t)/t <= lambda <= limsup K(t)/t as t -> oo, so if K(t)/t converges, its limit is lambda. Conjecture: K(t)/t converges.
%C A402125 A consequence of the rule: when a card is put back, all cards with smaller labels are above it. Hence, for each r >= 1, the cards are read for the r-th time in the order 1, 2, 3, ... of their labels. In the graph of the sequence the first readings, the second readings, the third readings, ... form a fan of bands that look like lines through the origin (see the plot in the Links section). If K(t)/t converges, then for each r >= 1 the ratio a(n)/n tends to b_r along the steps n at which a card is read for the r-th time, where b_r = H_r - H_(r-1) at x = lambda, that is, b_1 = lambda and b_(r+1) = b_r*(b_1 + ... + b_r); the numbers b_r add up to 1.
%C A402125 Other rules for putting the top card back give known sequences: below as many cards as its label, which gives the ruler function 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1, 4, ... (A001511; see Darnell and Dribus), or below as many cards as the number of earlier readings of this card (A355080). In the flashcard games of Novikoff, Kleinberg and Strogatz and of Lewis and Li the depth also depends on the number of readings. Here the depth is the time since the previous reading.
%C A402125 For each m >= 0, the coefficient of x^m in H_r becomes constant as r grows; these constants are A045761(m), and the values of H_r at x = 1 are A001696(r). See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
%H A402125 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402125/b402125.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A402125 Jesiah Darnell and Benjamin F. Dribus, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL28/Dribus/dribus4.html">Throwback sequences of positive integers</a>, J. Integer Seq. 28 (2025), Article 25.5.1.
%H A402125 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402125/a402125.txt">Notes, proofs and sources</a>.
%H A402125 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402125/a402125.png">Plot of a(n) for n = 1..30000</a>.
%H A402125 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402125/a402125_1.txt">Rust program</a>.
%H A402125 Joel Brewster Lewis and Nan Li, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1210.2419">Flashcard games</a>, arXiv:1210.2419 [math.CO], 2012-2013; published as Combinatorial aspects of flashcard games, Ann. Comb. 18 (2014), 459-472.
%H A402125 Timothy P. Novikoff, Jon M. Kleinberg and Steven H. Strogatz, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1109863109">Education of a model student</a>, Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA 109 (2012), 1868-1873.
%F A402125 a(n) = 1 if and only if n = r*(r+1)/2 for some r >= 1; a(n) = 2 if and only if n = r*(r+3)/2 for some r >= 1.
%F A402125 For j >= 1 and t >= 0, the number of m <= t with a(m) = j is at most floor((sqrt(8*t+1) - 1)/2), with equality for j = 1.
%F A402125 Let t_r(j) be the step at which card j is read for the r-th time (for example, t_1(1) = 1, t_2(1) = 3, t_3(1) = 6), and let S_r(t) be the number of steps m <= t at which the card read is being read for one of its first r times. Then S_r(t_(r+1)(j)) = t_r(j) + r for all r, j >= 1. For r = 1 this says: when card j is read for the second time, exactly t_1(j) + 1 distinct cards have been read.
%F A402125 liminf_{t->oo} K(t)/t <= lambda <= limsup_{t->oo} K(t)/t.
%F A402125 If K(t)/t converges, then lim_{j->oo} j/t_r(j) = b_r for every r >= 1, and Sum_{r>=1} b_r = 1.
%F A402125 For every fixed j >= 1, (number of m <= t with a(m) = j)/sqrt(2*t) tends to a positive rational number c_j as t -> oo; c_1 = c_2 = c_3 = 1 and c_4 = c_5 = c_6 = 1/2.
%e A402125 The deck after each of the first eight steps (the leftmost card is the top; k is the last earlier step at which the card was read, and g = n - k is the number of cards below which it is put back):
%e A402125   n  card  k  g  deck after step n
%e A402125   1    1   0  1  2,1,3,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,...
%e A402125   2    2   0  2  1,3,2,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,...
%e A402125   3    1   1  2  3,2,1,4,5,6,7,8,9,10,...
%e A402125   4    3   0  4  2,1,4,5,3,6,7,8,9,10,...
%e A402125   5    2   2  3  1,4,5,2,3,6,7,8,9,10,...
%e A402125   6    1   3  3  4,5,2,1,3,6,7,8,9,10,...
%e A402125   7    4   0  7  5,2,1,3,6,7,8,4,9,10,...
%e A402125   8    5   0  8  2,1,3,6,7,8,4,9,5,10,...
%e A402125 At step 5 the top card is 2. It was last read at step 2, so g = 5 - 2 = 3: removing it leaves 1,4,5,3,6,..., and putting it back below the first three of these cards gives 1,4,5,2,3,6,... . So a(1..8) = 1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5.
%o A402125 (Python)
%o A402125 # A402125: reinsert the top card after its elapsed age in remaining cards.
%o A402125 import sys
%o A402125 n = int(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 10000
%o A402125 deck = list(range(1, n + 2))
%o A402125 last = [0] * (n + 2)
%o A402125 for t in range(1, n + 1):
%o A402125     x = deck.pop(0)
%o A402125     deck.insert(t - last[x], x)
%o A402125     last[x] = t
%o A402125     print(x)
